// (Inv)-[:HAS_PART]->(Part)
// (Inv)-[:CONTAINS_SET]->(NestedSet)-[:HAS_INVENTORY]->(NestedInv)-[:HAS_PART]->(Part)
// (Inv)-[:CONTAINS_MINIFIG]->(Minifig)-[:HAS_INVENTORY]->(MinifigInv)-[:HAS_PART]->(Part)

// This is a tree structure. We can match paths starting from the root inventory.
// The path elements are:
// 1. (Inv)-[:HAS_PART]->(Part)
// 2. (Inv)-[:CONTAINS_SET]->(Set)-[:HAS_INVENTORY]->(Inv)-[:HAS_PART]->(Part)
// 3. (Inv)-[:CONTAINS_MINIFIG]->(Minifig)-[:HAS_INVENTORY]->(Inv)-[:HAS_PART]->(Part)
// And so on.

// A generic path from the root inventory to a part can be represented as:
// (rootInv:Inventory)-[path*1..10]->(part:Part)
// But the relationships alternate. 
